    <!-- **THE VIEWING WALL'S GLASS IS GONE, 0.12.87-dev.** Owner, 2026-10-04:
         "issues like the ballistic glass we used needs to be taken out of the starting
         scenerio to acturatley make sure it isnt needed to play the mod".

         This named RB_ReinforcedGlassWall and RB_GlassWall from ReBuild: Doors and
         Corners, register row 185, stance Optional. They were the ONLY two non-Core def
         references in any shipped start, and the genstep already fell back to a plain
         wall when ReBuild was absent, so nothing broke and nothing changes for a
         Core-only player: the same eleven cells were plain steel wall before and are
         plain steel wall now.

         What changes is that the claim is checkable. A shipped start that names another
         mod's defs cannot be audited as stand-alone by reading it; you have to go and
         read the C# that resolves it. check-register-compliance.py now refuses any
         non-Core def reference in a shipped start or scenario.

         The glazing field, the run plan and the genstep all stay: they cost nothing and
         a scenario may use them.

         **AND THE DIRECTION IT SERVED IS NOW ANSWERED, 0.12.98-dev.** Owner, 2026-10-04:
         "with like ballistic glass  walls for viewing the machine remotely and safely
         with security zones and shit". Measured against the installed game: Core ships
         NO barrier that is both see-through and sealing. Wall blocks sight; Barricade is
         passability PassThroughOnly at fillPercent 0.55, holds no roof and seals nothing,
         so a run of them across a twenty-cell hall breaks the seal AND reintroduces the
         roof-collapse case the pillars below exist to prevent.

         So "remotely" is answered by what already ships - the Operations Machine pane
         reads live gate state from any map - and "safely with security zones" by this
         facility's own shape: a solid wall plus the two-autodoor airlock above. That is
         strictly safer than a window, which is the half of the direction glass would
         have failed.

         A physical window remains a legitimate content decision and is recorded as an
         open row rather than left implied here. **The note this replaced said the
         direction "stays open in the queue" and it did NOT: no row for it existed in any
         queue, because the promise was written inside the row that closed and was
         archived with it. A sentence promising something stays open is not a queue
         row.** -->
